{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c4df","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0762f-28c8-71f3-b2cc-60a7b5a2a367"},"sequence":202,"recorded_at":1780531400000399,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"ce1e892e-3ef2-41ae-9338-dcad9ed9c1ee","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"ce1e892e-3ef2-41ae-9338-dcad9ed9c1ee","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"ce1e892e-3ef2-41ae-9338-dcad9ed9c1ee"},"call_id":"call_01a0762fb8f474f08c9cc3b41112371a","text":"<read-skill-result name=\"go-testing-tools\" status=\"ok\">\n<metadata>\npath: /Users/iv/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md\nlocator: file:///Users/iv/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools/SKILL.md\nsource: user\nbytes_returned: 15216\nbytes_total: 15216\ntruncated: false\n</metadata>\n\n<skill-body id=\"go-testing-tools\">\n---\nname: go-testing-tools\ndescription: &gt;\n  Go testing toolkit with tuitestkit library and a closed-loop development\n  cycle for Go applications. Covers test helpers, reducer tests,\n  snapshot/golden testing, mock executor patterns, and view assertions.\ntriggers:\n  - go test\n  - bubbletea test\n  - tuitestkit\n  - go test helpers\n  - reducer test\n  - snapshot test go\n  - golden file go\n  - тесты го\n  - го тест хелперы\n  - тесты баблти\n  - тест утилиты го\n  - замкнутый цикл тестирования\n---\n\n# Go Testing Tools\n\nTesting toolkit for Go TUI applications built with bubbletea. Provides a Go library (`tuitestkit`), reference documentation, and templates that enable a closed-loop development cycle: write code, write tests, run tests, validate, fix, repeat -- all without human intervention.\n\nCore philosophy: **Elm architecture makes TUI testable.** The reducer is a pure function -- given the same state and action, it always returns the same result. No I/O, no randomness, no terminal. Test everything as function calls.\n\n---\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- **Go 1.21+**\n- **bubbletea** (`github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea`)\n- **lipgloss** (`github.com/charmbracelet/lipgloss`) -- used by most bubbletea apps for styling\n\nInstall the library in your project:\n\n```bash\ngo get github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit\n```\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nMinimal test showing the core workflow: build a message, send it to a model, assert on the view.\n\n```go\npackage myscreen_test\n\nimport (\n    &quot;testing&quot;\n\n    &quot;github.com/relux-works/skill-go-testing-tools/tuitestkit&quot;\n)\n\nfunc TestBasicNavigation(t *testing.T) {\n    m := NewMyModel(testData)\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.WindowSize(80, 24))\n\n    // Press down twice\n    m = tuitestkit.Send(m, tuitestkit.Key(&quot;down&quot;), tuitestkit.Key(&quot;down&quot;))\n\n    // Assert the view contains expected content\n    tuitestkit.ViewContains(t, m, &quot;third item&quot;)\n    tuitestkit.ViewNotContains(t, m, &quot;ERROR&quot;)\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Testing Pyramid\n\nFive levels, from fastest/most numerous to slowest/fewest:\n\n| Level | What | Speed | Investment |\n|-------|------|-------|------------|\n| 1. Pure Reducer | `func(State, Action) State` | Nanoseconds | ~60% |\n| 2. Component | `Update` + `View` | Microseconds | ~20% |\n| 3. Integration | Multi-component with mocks | Milliseconds | ~10% |\n| 4. Snapshot | Golden file comparison | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n| 5. Behavioral | Full user workflows | Milliseconds | ~5% |\n\nWhen time is limited: **Level 1 &gt; Level 2 &gt; Level 3**. Pure reducer tests catch the most bugs per line of test code.\n\nFull details: `references/testing-pyramid.md`\n\n---\n\n## Library API Reference\n\nAll functions are in the `tuitestkit` package.\n\n### Message Builders (`messages.go`)\n\nBuild `tea.Msg` values for driving model updates.\n\n```go\n// Key builds a tea.KeyMsg from a human-readable string.\n// Supports: &quot;enter&quot;, &quot;tab&quot;, &quot;esc&quot;, &quot;space&quot;, &quot;backspace&quot;, &quot;up&quot;, &quot;down&quot;,\n// &quot;left&quot;, &quot;right&quot;, &quot;home&quot;, &quot;end&quot;, &quot;pgup&quot;, &quot;pgdown&quot;, &quot;delete&quot;, &quot;insert&quot;,\n// &quot;f1&quot;-&quot;f20&quot;, &quot;ctrl+c&quot;, &quot;ctrl+a&quot;...&quot;ctrl+z&quot;, &quot;alt+h&quot;, &quot;alt+enter&quot;,\n// single runes: &quot;a&quot;, &quot;/&quot;, &quot;1&quot;, etc.\nfunc Key(k string) tea.KeyMsg\n\n// Keys builds a slice of tea.Msg from multiple key strings.\nfunc Keys(keys ...string) []tea.Msg\n\n// WindowSize builds a tea.WindowSizeMsg with the given dimensions.\nfunc WindowSize(w, h int) tea.WindowSizeMsg\n\n// MouseClick builds a tea.MouseMsg for a left-button click at (x, y).\nfunc MouseClick(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseClickRight builds a tea.MouseMsg for a right-button click at (x, y).\nfunc MouseClickRight(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseScroll builds a tea.MouseMsg for a scroll event.\n// Direction constants: ScrollUp, ScrollDown, ScrollLeft, ScrollRight.\nfunc MouseScroll(dir ScrollDir) tea.MouseMsg\n\n// MouseRelease builds a tea.MouseMsg for a button release at (x, y).\nfunc MouseRelease(x, y int) tea.MouseMsg\n```\n\n### Model Harness (`harness.go`)\n\nSend messages to bubbletea models and handle commands.\n\n```go\n// Send sends messages to a model sequentially, returns the final model.\n// Preserves concrete type via generics -- no type assertion needed.\n// Panics if Update returns a different type (broken implementation).\n// Init() is NOT called.\nfunc Send[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) M\n\n// SendAndCollect sends messages, collects all non-nil Cmds returned by Update.\n// Returns final model + collected commands.\nfunc SendAndCollect[M tea.Model](model M, msgs ...tea.Msg) (M, []tea.Cmd)\n\n// ExecCmds executes tea.Cmd functions synchronously, collects resulting messages.\n// Recursively handles tea.BatchMsg. Nil cmds/messages are skipped.\nfunc ExecCmds(cmds ...tea.Cmd) []tea.Msg\n```\n\n**Common pattern -- command pipeline:**\n\n```go\nm, cmds := tuitestkit.SendAndCollect(m, tuitestkit.Key(&quot;r&quot;))\nmsgs := tuitestkit.ExecCmds(cmds...)\nm = tuitestkit.Send(m, msgs...)\n```\n\n### Reducer Harness (`reducer.go`)\n\nTable-driven testing for pure reducer functions.\n\n```go\n// ReducerTest defines a single test case for a pure reducer.\n// S = state type, A = action type.\ntype ReducerTest[S any, A any] struct {\n    Name    string\n    Initial S\n    Action  A\n    Assert  func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// Step defines one step in a multi-action reducer sequence.\n// Assert is optional -- if nil, applied without per-step validation.\ntype Step[S any, A any] struct {\n    Name   string\n    Action A\n    Assert func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// ReducerSequence defines a multi-step test scenario.\n// Actions applied sequentially from Initial. Final runs on end state.\ntype ReducerSequence[S, A any] struct {\n    Name    string\n    Initial S\n    Steps   []Step[S, A]\n    Final   func(t *testing.T, got S)\n}\n\n// RunReducerTests executes table-driven reducer tests as subtests.\nfunc RunReducerTests[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, tests []ReducerTest[S, A])\n\n// RunReducerSequences executes multi-step sequence tests.\n// Per-step Assert runs after each step (if non-nil). Final runs on end state.\nfunc RunReducerSequences[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, sequences []ReducerSequence[S, A])\n```\n\n**Invariant checking:**\n\n```go\n// Invariant defines a property that must hold for any state.\ntype Invariant[S any] struct {\n    Name  string\n    Check func(s S) error\n}\n\n// InvariantChecker holds invariants and validates state against all of them.\ntype InvariantChecker[S any] struct { /* ... */ }\n\n// NewInvariantChecker creates a checker with the given invariants.\nfunc NewInvariantChecker[S any](invariants ...Invariant[S]) *InvariantChecker[S]\n\n// Check validates state against all invariants. Returns combined error or nil.\nfunc (ic *InvariantChecker[S]) Check(s S) error\n\n// WrapWithInvariants wraps a reducer with invariant checking.\n// After every reduce call, all invariants are validated.\n// t.Fatalf on violation.\nfunc WrapWithInvariants[S, A any](t *testing.T, reduce func(S, A) S, checker *InvariantChecker[S]) func(S, A) S\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\n```go\nchecker := tuitestkit.NewInvariantChecker(\n    tuitestkit.Invariant[AppState]{\n        Name: &quot;cursor in bounds&quot;,\n        Check: func(s AppState) error {\n            if s.Cursor &lt; 0 || s.Cursor &gt;= len(s.Items) {\n                return fmt.Errorf(&quot;cursor %d out of [0, %d)&quot;, s.Cursor, len(s.Items))\n            }\n            return nil\n        },\n    },\n)\nsafeReduce := tuitestkit.WrapWithInvariants(t, Reduce, checker)\ntuitestkit.RunReducerTests(t, safeReduce, tests)\n```\n\n### Mock Building Blocks (`mock.go`)\n\nComposable primitives for building project-specific mocks.\n\n```go\n// MockCall represents a single recorded method invocation.\ntype MockCall struct {\n    Method string\n    Args   []any\n}\n\n// MockCallRecorder provides thread-safe recording of method calls.\n// Embed into project-specific mock structs.\ntype MockCallRecorder struct { /* ... */ }\n\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Record(method string, args ...any)\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallCount(method string) int\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Calls() []MockCall\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) CallsFor(method string) []MockCall\nfunc (r *MockCallRecorder) Reset()\n\n// MockResponseMap provides thread-safe canned response storage.\ntype MockResponseMap struct { /* ... */ }\n\nfunc NewMockResponseMap() *MockResponseMap\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Set(key string, data []byte, err error)\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) Get(key string) ([]byte, error)\nfunc (m *MockResponseMap) SetError(key string, err error)\n\n// Assertion helpers\nfunc AssertCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\nfunc AssertNotCalled(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string)\nfunc AssertCalledN(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, n int)\nfunc AssertCalledWith(t testing.TB, r *MockCallRecorder, method string, args ...any)\n```\n\n**Building a mock (two lines per method):**\n\n```go\ntype TaskBoardMock struct {\n    tuitestkit.MockCallRecorder\n    Responses *tuitestkit.MockResponseMap\n}\n\nfunc NewTaskBoardMock() *TaskBoardMock {\n    return &amp;TaskBoardMock{Responses: tuitestkit.NewMockResponseMap()}\n}\n\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) TreeJSON() ([]byte, error) {\n    m.Record(&quot;TreeJSON&quot;)\n    return m.Responses.Get(&quot;TreeJSON&quot;)\n}\n\nfunc (m *TaskBoardMock) Execute(cmd string, args ...string) ([]byte, error) {\n    m.Record(&quot;Execute&quot;, cmd, args)\n    return m.Responses.Get(&quot;Execute:&quot; + cmd)\n}\n```\n\n### View Assertions (`view.go`)\n\nAssert on rendered output. All functions strip ANSI escape codes before comparison.\n\n```go\n// StripANSI removes all ANSI escape sequences from a string.\nfunc StripANSI(s string) string\n\n// --- Model-based (call model.View() internally) ---\n\nfunc ViewContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\nfunc ViewNotContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, text string)\nfunc ViewLines(model tea.Model) []string\nfunc ViewLineContains(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\nfunc ViewLineEquals(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, lineIdx int, text string)\nfunc ViewMatchesRegex(t testing.TB, model tea.Model, pattern string)\n\n// --- String-based (when you already have the view string) ---\n\nfunc ContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\nfunc NotContainsStr(t testing.TB, view string, text string)\nfunc LinesFromStr(view string) []string\nfunc MatchesRegexStr(t testing.TB, view string, pattern string)\n```\n\n### Snapshot Testing (`snapshot.go`)\n\nGolden file comparison for visual regression testing.\n\n```go\n// UpdateSnapshots controls whether snapshots are written (true) or compared (false).\n// Set via UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 environment variable.\nvar UpdateSnapshots bool\n\n// SnapshotView captures model.View(), strips ANSI, compares against golden file.\nfunc SnapshotView(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\n\n// SnapshotViewRaw captures model.View() with raw ANSI codes intact.\nfunc SnapshotViewRaw(t *testing.T, model tea.Model, name string)\n\n// SnapshotStr compares a pre-rendered string (ANSI-stripped) against golden file.\nfunc SnapshotStr(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\n\n// SnapshotStrRaw compares a pre-rendered string (raw ANSI) against golden file.\nfunc SnapshotStrRaw(t *testing.T, view string, name string)\n```\n\nGolden files stored at `testdata/snapshots/&lt;name&gt;.golden` relative to the test file.\n\n```bash\n# Create or update golden files\nUPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./...\n\n# Update for a single package\nUPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 go test ./internal/ui/screens/board/ -run TestSnapshot\n```\n\nMismatches produce a line-by-line diff with `-`/`+` markers.\n\n---\n\n## Closed-Loop Workflow\n\nThe agent development cycle for writing tests:\n\n```\n1. UNDERSTAND: Read screen/component code\n2. IDENTIFY:   What behaviors need tests?\n3. EXTRACT:    If exec.Command present, extract to interface (use mock template)\n4. WRITE:      Tests using tuitestkit helpers\n5. RUN:        go test ./... -v\n6. VALIDATE:   All pass? Check coverage with go test -cover\n7. FIX:        If failures, read error, fix code or test, goto 5\n8. SNAPSHOT:   Optionally capture view golden files\n9. DONE:       Tests green, coverage acceptable\n```\n\n### Step Details\n\n**UNDERSTAND:** Read the component source. Identify:\n- What state does it manage?\n- What messages does it handle in Update?\n- Does it have a pure reducer (`func(State, Action) State`)?\n- Does it shell out to external commands (exec.Command)?\n- What does View() render?\n\n**IDENTIFY:** Map behaviors to test levels:\n- Pure state logic -&gt; Level 1 (reducer tests)\n- Key bindings, view rendering -&gt; Level 2 (component tests)\n- Data loading, command execution -&gt; Level 3 (integration tests with mocks)\n- Visual appearance -&gt; Level 4 (snapshot tests)\n\n**EXTRACT:** If the component uses `exec.Command` or any I/O directly:\n1. Define an interface with only the methods this component needs\n2. Move `exec.Command` calls to a production implementation\n3. Build a mock using `MockCallRecorder` + `MockResponseMap`\n4. See `assets/templates/executor_interface.go.tmpl` and `assets/templates/mock_executor.go.tmpl`\n\n**WRITE:** Use the appropriate tuitestkit helpers per level:\n- Level 1: `RunReducerTests`, `RunReducerSequences`, `WrapWithInvariants`\n- Level 2: `Send`, `ViewContains`, `ViewLines`, `Key`, `MouseClick`\n- Level 3: `SendAndCollect`, `ExecCmds`, `AssertCalled`, `AssertCalledWith`\n- Level 4: `SnapshotView`, `SnapshotStr`\n\n**RUN:** `go test ./... -v` -- all tests must run in under 1 second (no I/O, no network).\n\n**VALIDATE:** `go test ./... -cover` -- target 80%+ for code under test.\n\n**FIX:** Read error messages carefully. Common issues:\n- Type assertion panic -&gt; `Update` returns wrong type (broken implementation)\n- View assertion failure -&gt; check ANSI stripping, check exact text\n- Snapshot mismatch -&gt; run with `UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1` if the change is intentional\n- Mock assertion failure -&gt; check response key matches (`&quot;Execute:list&quot;` not `&quot;Execute&quot;`)\n\n---\n\n## Templates\n\nScaffolding templates in `assets/templates/`:\n\n| Template | Purpose |\n|----------|---------|\n| `reducer_test.go.tmpl` | Table-driven reducer tests with invariants |\n| `component_test.go.tmpl` | Component model tests (keyboard, mouse, commands, view) |\n| `snapshot_test.go.tmpl` | Snapshot golden file tests |\n| `executor_interface.go.tmpl` | Executor interface + production stub |\n| `mock_executor.go.tmpl` | Mock executor using MockCallRecorder + MockResponseMap |\n| `PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md` | Recommended directory layout for test files |\n\nCopy templates into your project, replace `TODO` markers with your actual types and import paths.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\nIn-depth documentation in `references/`:\n\n| Document | Content |\n|----------|---------|\n| `elm-architecture.md` | Why Elm makes TUI testable, state/action/reducer/view cycle, nested reducers, dependency injection, the executor pattern |\n| `mock-patterns.md` | Interface extraction, when to mock vs test pure functions, stubs vs mocks vs fakes, callback capture, full mock lifecycle example |\n| `testing-pyramid.md` | All 5 test levels with code examples, decision table for which level to use, coverage strategy |\n\n---\n\n## Setup\n\nRun the setup script to create symlinks:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/setup.sh\n```\n\nThis links the skill to `~/.agents/skills/go-testing-tools`, `~/.claude/skills/go-testing-tools`, and `~/.codex/skills/go-testing-tools`.\n\nTo remove:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/deinit.sh\n```\n\n</skill-body>\n</read-skill-result>","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_skill","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c4e1","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0762f-28c8-71f3-b2cc-60a7b5a2a367"},"sequence":203,"recorded_at":1780531400000401,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"ce1e892e-3ef2-41ae-9338-dcad9ed9c1ee","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"ce1e892e-3ef2-41ae-9338-dcad9ed9c1ee","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"ce1e892e-3ef2-41ae-9338-dcad9ed9c1ee"},"call_id":"call_01a0762fb9897ee0a525d53a278ad11e","text":"Read text file `internal/environ/doc.go`.\n1|// Package environ is the single environment library behind the\n2|// Provider, Session Adapter, and Directory Node facades.\n3|//\n4|// Pinned specification v0.5.0 requires the Directory Node facade to\n5|// expose source-local environment discovery \"through the same\n6|// parsers, identity logic, redaction rules, tuple gates, and\n7|// fixtures as the Provider and Session Adapter facades\" (Section\n8|// 3.1), backed by \"the same environment implementation as Provider\n9|// 2 and Session Adapter 1\" (Sections 7.9, 10.8, 13.14). Before this\n10|// package each facade carried its own copy of those rules, and\n11|// independent copies of the same rule drift in ways no per-facade\n12|// reviewer can see. This package is the one implementation every\n13|// facade must converge on:\n14|//\n15|//   - frame parsing: DecodeStrictObject is the only JSON entry\n16|//     point, with duplicate-member rejection and the Section 1.6\n17|//     lone-surrogate gate in canonical string-walk semantics;\n18|//   - string measure: CheckStringBounds counts Unicode characters\n19|//     (runes), never bytes and never UTF-16 code units;\n20|//   - shared grammars: the environment-id, provider-id (via\n21|//     scalar), SemVer, platform, and architecture rules;\n22|//   - tuple gates: DecodeTuple admits the exact six-member\n23|//     Environment Tuple of Sections 7.8 and 13.14, which never\n24|//     carries executable provenance or extensions;\n25|//   - observation gates: DecodeEnvironmentObservation admits the\n26|//     Section 10.8.1 Environment Observation, reusing the tuple\n27|//     admission model rather than creating a second one.\n28|//\n29|// Every refusal is a plain error whose text names the rule and the\n30|// member: facades keep their own refusal dialects (Structured\n31|// Error codes per protocol), so this package names rules instead\n32|// of minting codes, and the boundary battery pins each facade's\n33|// rendering separately.\n34|//\n35|// Stated bounds. Provider Identity refusal dialects stay in\n36|// provhost and canonicaljson: the two validators must agree\n37|// rule-for-rule and the agreement battery in this package pins\n38|// that, but a third validator here would be another copy, not\n39|// convergence. Byte-counted exec rules (Provider SpawnPlan argv\n40|// and env literals, Section 5.1) stay in provhost: they bound\n41|// bytes by specification while every character-bound member in\n42|// this story counts runes, and the measure battery ledgers that\n43|// split. The tuple-registry signature, sequence monotonicity, and\n44|// publication rules of Section 13.14 fail closed at the registry\n45|// owner, which does not exist yet. This package mutates no durable\n46|// state and keeps no cache: every entry point is a pure function\n47|// of its input bytes.\n48|package environ","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c4e3","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0762f-28c8-71f3-b2cc-60a7b5a2a367"},"sequence":204,"recorded_at":1780531400000403,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"ce1e892e-3ef2-41ae-9338-dcad9ed9c1ee","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"ce1e892e-3ef2-41ae-9338-dcad9ed9c1ee","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"ce1e892e-3ef2-41ae-9338-dcad9ed9c1ee"},"call_id":"call_01a0762fba3b7f319683774269e54db2","text":"Read text file `internal/environ/decode.go`.\n1|package environ\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"bytes\"\n5|\t\"encoding/json\"\n6|\t\"fmt\"\n7|\t\"io\"\n8|\t\"regexp\"\n9|\t\"strconv\"\n10|\t\"strings\"\n11|\t\"unicode/utf8\"\n12|\n13|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/scalar\"\n14|)\n15|\n16|// maxUint53 is the largest exactly representable JSON integer:\n17|// counts and limits are uint53 throughout the pinned\n18|// specification.\n19|const maxUint53 = uint64(1<<53 - 1)\n20|\n21|// Fault is one strict-decode failure: what was wrong and which\n22|// member it was found in, if any. It never carries foreign bytes.\n23|// The five details are the complete fault space: every body this\n24|// package refuses at the frame layer carries exactly one of them,\n25|// and the frame-agreement battery derives its denominator from\n26|// this list, so a sixth fault shape fails the census instead of\n27|// passing silently.\n28|type Fault struct {\n29|\tDetail string\n30|\tMember string\n31|}\n32|\n33|func (fault *Fault) Error() string {\n34|\tif fault.Member == \"\" {\n35|\t\treturn \"environment frame \" + fault.Detail\n36|\t}\n37|\treturn \"environment frame \" + fault.Detail + \" in member \" + fault.Member\n38|}\n39|\n40|// Frame fault details. Each is a constant so the agreement battery\n41|// derives the fault space from production source instead of\n42|// retyping it: a reworded fault fails its row rather than passing\n43|// against a stale copy.\n44|const (\n45|\tFaultNotUTF8       = \"not valid UTF-8\"\n46|\tFaultLoneSurrogate = \"lone surrogate escape\"\n47|\tFaultNotObject     = \"not a JSON object\"\n48|\tFaultDuplicate     = \"duplicate member\"\n49|\tFaultTrailing      = \"trailing data after the object\"\n50|)\n51|\n52|// DecodeStrictObject parses one complete JSON object with\n53|// duplicate member rejection. It is the only JSON entry point in\n54|// this package: every body crosses it before any member is read.\n55|// A non-UTF-8 frame, a lone surrogate escape, a non-object top\n56|// level, a duplicated member, or trailing data is a fault, never\n57|// a partial object the caller could mistake for a result.\n58|func DecodeStrictObject(data []byte) (map[string]json.RawMessage, *Fault) {\n59|\tif !utf8.Valid(data) {\n60|\t\treturn nil, &Fault{Detail: FaultNotUTF8}\n61|\t}\n62|\tif HasLoneSurrogateEscape(data) {\n63|\t\treturn nil, &Fault{Detail: FaultLoneSurrogate}\n64|\t}\n65|\tdecoder := json.NewDecoder(bytes.NewReader(data))\n66|\ttoken, err := decoder.Token()\n67|\tif err != nil {\n68|\t\treturn nil, &Fault{Detail: FaultNotObject}\n69|\t}\n70|\tdelim, ok := token.(json.Delim)\n71|\tif !ok || delim != '{' {\n72|\t\treturn nil, &Fault{Detail: FaultNotObject}\n73|\t}\n74|\tmembers := map[string]json.RawMessage{}\n75|\tfor decoder.More() {\n76|\t\tkeyToken, err := decoder.Token()\n77|\t\tif err != nil {\n78|\t\t\treturn nil, &Fault{Detail: FaultNotObject}\n79|\t\t}\n80|\t\tkey, ok := keyToken.(string)\n81|\t\tif !ok {\n82|\t\t\treturn nil, &Fault{Detail: FaultNotObject}\n83|\t\t}\n84|\t\tif _, duplicate := members[key]; duplicate {\n85|\t\t\treturn nil, &Fault{Detail: FaultDuplicate, Member: key}\n86|\t\t}\n87|\t\tvar raw json.RawMessage\n88|\t\tif err := decoder.Decode(&raw); err != nil {\n89|\t\t\treturn nil, &Fault{Detail: FaultNotObject, Member: key}\n90|\t\t}\n91|\t\tmembers[key] = raw\n92|\t}\n93|\tif token, err := decoder.Token(); err != nil {\n94|\t\treturn nil, &Fault{Detail: FaultNotObject}\n95|\t} else if delim, ok := token.(json.Delim); !ok || delim != '}' {\n96|\t\treturn nil, &Fault{Detail: FaultNotObject}\n97|\t}\n98|\tif _, err := decoder.Token(); err != io.EOF {\n99|\t\treturn nil, &Fault{Detail: FaultTrailing}\n100|\t}\n101|\treturn members, nil\n102|}\n103|\n104|// HasLoneSurrogateEscape reports whether the raw frame carries a\n105|// UTF-16 escape the JSON string grammar cannot pair. Go's\n106|// encoding/json would otherwise replace the lone escape with\n107|// U+FFFD silently, so wire bodies carrying lone surrogates would\n108|// be admitted with rewritten bytes.\n109|//\n110|// The walk is string-aware: only escapes inside JSON strings\n111|// count, and a backslash escape consumes both bytes, so the text\n112|// \"\\\\ud800\" (backslash-plus-text, JSON source `\"\\\\ud800\"`) is\n113|// not an escape and is admitted. A scanner that matches every\n114|// `\\u` byte pair regardless of string context refuses that text:\n115|// that false positive is exactly the recorded sessadapter and\n116|// dirnode divergence the frame-agreement battery ledgers, and any\n117|// change to it fails there in either direction.\n118|func HasLoneSurrogateEscape(data []byte) bool {\n119|\tfor index := 0; index < len(data); index++ {\n120|\t\tif data[index] != '\"' {\n121|\t\t\tcontinue\n122|\t\t}\n123|\t\tfor index++; index < len(data); index++ {\n124|\t\t\tswitch data[index] {\n125|\t\t\tcase '\"':\n126|\t\t\t\tgoto stringComplete\n127|\t\t\tcase '\\\\':\n128|\t\t\t\tindex++\n129|\t\t\t\tif index >= len(data) || data[index] != 'u' {\n130|\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n131|\t\t\t\t}\n132|\t\t\t\tunit, end, ok := readUTF16Escape(data, index-1)\n133|\t\t\t\tif !ok {\n134|\t\t\t\t\t// Not a well-formed \\uXXXX escape: the\n135|\t\t\t\t\t// JSON-syntax arms own it, not this gate.\n136|\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n137|\t\t\t\t}\n138|\t\t\t\tindex = end - 1\n139|\t\t\t\tswitch {\n140|\t\t\t\tcase unit >= 0xd800 && unit <= 0xdbff:\n141|\t\t\t\t\tsecond, _, ok := readUTF16Escape(data, end)\n142|\t\t\t\t\tif !ok || second < 0xdc00 || second > 0xdfff {\n143|\t\t\t\t\t\treturn true\n144|\t\t\t\t\t}\n145|\t\t\t\t\tindex = end + 6 - 1\n146|\t\t\t\tcase unit >= 0xdc00 && unit <= 0xdfff:\n147|\t\t\t\t\treturn true\n148|\t\t\t\t}\n149|\t\t\t}\n150|\t\t}\n151|\t\t// An unterminated string owns no surrogate verdict: the\n152|\t\t// frame is not JSON at all, so the syntax arms refuse it\n153|\t\t// as not-a-JSON-object rather than misattributing it\n154|\t\t// here.\n155|\t\treturn false\n156|\tstringComplete:\n157|\t}\n158|\treturn false\n159|}\n160|\n161|// readUTF16Escape reads one \\uXXXX escape starting at the offset.\n162|// It reports the unit value and the offset just past the escape,\n163|// or false when the escape is truncated or not hexadecimal.\n164|func readUTF16Escape(data []byte, offset int) (uint16, int, bool) {\n165|\tif offset+6 > len(data) || data[offset] != '\\\\' || data[offset+1] != 'u' {\n166|\t\treturn 0, offset, false\n167|\t}\n168|\tvalue, err := strconv.ParseUint(string(data[offset+2:offset+6]), 16, 16)\n169|\tif err != nil {\n170|\t\treturn 0, offset, false\n171|\t}\n172|\treturn uint16(value), offset + 6, true\n173|}\n174|\n175|// unknownMember names a member outside the closed set, if any.\n176|func unknownMember(members map[string]json.RawMessage, allowed map[string]bool) (string, bool) {\n177|\tfor name := range members {\n178|\t\tif !allowed[name] {\n179|\t\t\treturn name, true\n180|\t\t}\n181|\t}\n182|\treturn \"\", false\n183|}\n184|\n185|// missingMember names the first required member with no value, if\n186|// any. The required list is fixed order, so the verdict is\n187|// deterministic for every missing combination.\n188|func missingMember(members map[string]json.RawMessage, required []string) (string, bool) {\n189|\tfor _, name := range required {\n190|\t\tif _, present := members[name]; !present {\n191|\t\t\treturn name, true\n192|\t\t}\n193|\t}\n194|\treturn \"\", false\n195|}\n196|\n197|// rawString reads a JSON string member.\n198|func rawString(raw json.RawMessage) (string, bool) {\n199|\tvar value string\n200|\tif err := json.Unmarshal(raw, &value); err != nil {\n201|\t\treturn \"\", false\n202|\t}\n203|\treturn value, true\n204|}\n205|\n206|// rawUint53 reads an unsigned integral JSON number in [0,\n207|// 2^53-1]. A fraction, exponent, sign, or out-of-range magnitude\n208|// is not a uint53, even when it names the same mathematical\n209|// value: the bodies carry counts, never measurements.\n210|func rawUint53(raw json.RawMessage) (uint64, bool) {\n211|\tdecoder := json.NewDecoder(bytes.NewReader(raw))\n212|\tdecoder.UseNumber()\n213|\tvar value any\n214|\tif err := decoder.Decode(&value); err != nil {\n215|\t\treturn 0, false\n216|\t}\n217|\tnumber, ok := value.(json.Number)\n218|\tif !ok {\n219|\t\treturn 0, false\n220|\t}\n221|\tliteral := number.String()\n222|\tif literal == \"\" || strings.ContainsAny(literal, \".eE+-\") {\n223|\t\treturn 0, false\n224|\t}\n225|\treturn parseUint53Literal(literal)\n226|}\n227|\n228|// parseUint53Literal reads a digit-only literal without trusting\n229|// a float conversion: the magnitude bound is checked on the digit\n230|// string, so 2^53 is refused even on platforms that could hold\n231|// it.\n232|func parseUint53Literal(literal string) (uint64, bool) {\n233|\tvar value uint64\n234|\tfor index := 0; index < len(literal); index++ {\n235|\t\tdigit := literal[index]\n236|\t\tif digit < '0' || digit > '9' {\n237|\t\t\treturn 0, false\n238|\t\t}\n239|\t\tvalue = value*10 + uint64(digit-'0')\n240|\t\tif value > maxUint53 {\n241|\t\t\treturn 0, false\n242|\t\t}\n243|\t}\n244|\treturn value, true\n245|}\n246|\n247|// StringLength is the Section 1.6 string measure in characters,\n248|// not bytes: a bound of string[1..128] admits 128 characters of\n249|// any width. Byte-counted exec rules (Provider SpawnPlan argv and\n250|// env literals, Section 5.1) do not go through here; the measure\n251|// battery ledgers that split.\n252|func StringLength(value string) int {\n253|\treturn utf8.RuneCountInString(value)\n254|}\n255|\n256|// CheckStringBounds reports whether the member is a JSON string\n257|// whose character count falls in the inclusive bound.\n258|func CheckStringBounds(raw json.RawMessage, minimum, maximum int) (string, bool) {\n259|\tvalue, ok := rawString(raw)\n260|\tif !ok {\n261|\t\treturn \"\", false\n262|\t}\n263|\tlength := StringLength(value)\n264|\tif length < minimum || length > maximum {\n265|\t\treturn \"\", false\n266|\t}\n267|\treturn value, true\n268|}\n269|\n270|// CheckUint53Bounds reports whether the member is a uint53 in the\n271|// inclusive bound. Both edges are checked: a one-sided check\n272|// would admit the open side.\n273|func CheckUint53Bounds(raw json.RawMessage, minimum, maximum uint64) (uint64, bool) {\n274|\tvalue, ok := rawUint53(raw)\n275|\tif !ok {\n276|\t\treturn 0, false\n277|\t}\n278|\tif value < minimum || value > maximum {\n279|\t\treturn 0, false\n280|\t}\n281|\treturn value, true\n282|}\n283|\n284|// CheckDigest reports whether the member is a digest of the\n285|// pinned sha256 form.\n286|func CheckDigest(raw json.RawMessage) (scalar.Digest, bool) {\n287|\tvalue, ok := rawString(raw)\n288|\tif !ok {\n289|\t\treturn scalar.Digest{}, false\n290|\t}\n291|\tdigest, err := scalar.ParseDigest(value)\n292|\tif err != nil {\n293|\t\treturn scalar.Digest{}, false\n294|\t}\n295|\treturn digest, true\n296|}\n297|\n298|// CheckUUIDv7 reports whether the member is a lowercase UUIDv7.\n299|func CheckUUIDv7(raw json.RawMessage) (scalar.UUIDv7, bool) {\n300|\tvalue, ok := rawString(raw)\n301|\tif !ok {\n302|\t\treturn scalar.UUIDv7{}, false\n303|\t}\n304|\tidentifier, err := scalar.ParseUUIDv7(value)\n305|\tif err != nil {\n306|\t\treturn scalar.UUIDv7{}, false\n307|\t}\n308|\treturn identifier, true\n309|}\n310|\n311|// CheckTimestamp reports whether the member is a pinned\n312|// timestamp.\n313|func CheckTimestamp(raw json.RawMessage) (scalar.Timestamp, bool) {\n314|\tvalue, ok := rawString(raw)\n315|\tif !ok {\n316|\t\treturn scalar.Timestamp{}, false\n317|\t}\n318|\tinstant, err := scalar.ParseTimestamp(value)\n319|\tif err != nil {\n320|\t\treturn scalar.Timestamp{}, false\n321|\t}\n322|\treturn instant, true\n323|}\n324|\n325|// isNull reports whether the member is an explicit JSON null.\n326|func isNull(raw json.RawMessage) bool {\n327|\treturn string(bytes.TrimSpace(raw)) == \"null\"\n328|}\n329|\n330|func bytesTrimSpace(raw json.RawMessage) []byte {\n331|\treturn bytes.TrimSpace(raw)\n332|}\n333|\n334|// reverseDNSPattern is the extensions-key grammar: at least two\n335|// dot-separated labels, each starting with a lowercase letter.\n336|var reverseDNSPattern = regexp.MustCompile(`^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,62}(\\.[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,62})+$`)\n337|\n338|// CheckExtensions reports whether the member is an object whose\n339|// every key is reverse-DNS. Extensions never add operations,\n340|// capabilities, or trust facts; the key grammar is the part of\n341|// that rule decidable from one body.\n342|func CheckExtensions(raw json.RawMessage) bool {\n343|\tmembers, fault := DecodeStrictObject(bytesTrimSpace(raw))\n344|\tif fault != nil {\n345|\t\treturn false\n346|\t}\n347|\tfor name := range members {\n348|\t\tif len(name) < 3 || len(name) > 253 || !reverseDNSPattern.MatchString(name) {\n349|\t\t\treturn false\n350|\t\t}\n351|\t}\n352|\treturn true\n353|}\n354|\n355|// decodeArray reads a JSON array member into its raw elements. A\n356|// non-array — including null — is not an empty array.\n357|func decodeArray(raw json.RawMessage) ([]json.RawMessage, bool) {\n358|\tdecoder := json.NewDecoder(bytes.NewReader(raw))\n359|\ttoken, err := decoder.Token()\n360|\tif err != nil {\n361|\t\treturn nil, false\n362|\t}\n363|\tdelim, ok := token.(json.Delim)\n364|\tif !ok || delim != '[' {\n365|\t\treturn nil, false\n366|\t}\n367|\tvar elements []json.RawMessage\n368|\tfor decoder.More() {\n369|\t\tvar element json.RawMessage\n370|\t\tif err := decoder.Decode(&element); err != nil {\n371|\t\t\treturn nil, false\n372|\t\t}\n373|\t\telements = append(elements, element)\n374|\t}\n375|\tif token, err := decoder.Token(); err != nil {\n376|\t\treturn nil, false\n377|\t} else if delim, ok := token.(json.Delim); !ok || delim != ']' {\n378|\t\treturn nil, false\n379|\t}\n380|\tif _, err := decoder.Token(); err != io.EOF {\n381|\t\treturn nil, false\n382|\t}\n383|\treturn elements, true\n384|}\n385|\n386|// CheckSortedUniqueStrings reports whether the member is an array\n387|// of strings in the item bound whose JCS encodings order strictly\n388|// increasingly with no duplicates. Bytewise JCS order over\n389|// strings is plain lexicographic order, so the check compares\n390|// directly; the count bound is checked on both edges.\n391|//\n392|// Ordering and uniqueness are two obligations, not one: an\n393|// unsorted-but-unique vector and a sorted-but-duplicated vector\n394|// are separate rows in the observation battery, so a check that\n395|// enforced only one half could not pass both.\n396|func CheckSortedUniqueStrings(raw json.RawMessage, minimumLength, maximumLength int, minimumCount, maximumCount uint64) ([]string, bool) {\n397|\telements, ok := decodeArray(raw)\n398|\tif !ok {\n399|\t\treturn nil, false\n400|\t}\n401|\tif uint64(len(elements)) < minimumCount || uint64(len(elements)) > maximumCount {\n402|\t\treturn nil, false\n403|\t}\n404|\tvalues := make([]string, 0, len(elements))\n405|\tfor _, element := range elements {\n406|\t\tvalue, ok := CheckStringBounds(element, minimumLength, maximumLength)\n407|\t\tif !ok {\n408|\t\t\treturn nil, false\n409|\t\t}\n410|\t\tvalues = append(values, value)\n411|\t}\n412|\tfor index := 1; index < len(values); index++ {\n413|\t\tif values[index-1] >= values[index] {\n414|\t\t\treturn nil, false\n415|\t\t}\n416|\t}\n417|\treturn values, true\n418|}\n419|\n420|// CheckSortedUniqueDigests reports whether the member is an array\n421|// of digests in the count bound, ordered strictly increasingly\n422|// with no duplicates.\n423|func CheckSortedUniqueDigests(raw json.RawMessage, minimumCount, maximumCount uint64) ([]scalar.Digest, bool) {\n424|\telements, ok := decodeArray(raw)\n425|\tif !ok {\n426|\t\treturn nil, false\n427|\t}\n428|\tif uint64(len(elements)) < minimumCount || uint64(len(elements)) > maximumCount {\n429|\t\treturn nil, false\n430|\t}\n431|\tvalues := make([]scalar.Digest, 0, len(elements))\n432|\tfor _, element := range elements {\n433|\t\tdigest, ok := CheckDigest(element)\n434|\t\tif !ok {\n435|\t\t\treturn nil, false\n436|\t\t}\n437|\t\tvalues = append(values, digest)\n438|\t}\n439|\tfor index := 1; index < len(values); index++ {\n440|\t\tif values[index-1].String() >= values[index].String() {\n441|\t\t\treturn nil, false\n442|\t\t}\n443|\t}\n444|\treturn values, true\n445|}\n446|\n447|// refuse builds the package error for one refused member: the\n448|// rule in human text automation must never branch on, naming the\n449|// member. Tests assert the full text, so a reworded rule fails\n450|// its row instead of passing against a stale copy.\n451|func refuse(rule, member string) error {\n452|\tif member == \"\" {\n453|\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"environment %s\", rule)\n454|\t}\n455|\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"environment %s: %s\", rule, member)\n456|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
